//? Title: Goertzel Power: Actual States, Selected Frequencies and Numerical Limits
//? Subtitle: A native C++ study of a four-sample calculation and synthetic keypad candidates
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a finite real signal, measure its Fourier response at one or a few chosen
frequencies without storing a full transform. A two-state recurrence provides
that estimate, but selecting the right frequency and interpreting its output
still matter. This report follows four samples by hand, distinguishes historical
bin snapping from direct frequency evaluation, and reproduces the original
770+1336 Hz keypad illustration through native signal helpers. It also retains
quiet and incomplete-tone counterexamples, measured work and finite cancellation
errors. A maximum-response keypad candidate is not presented as a validated receiver.
)

\toc

> Four samples and two carried states

Let x=[1,2,3,4], N=4 and k=0. The angular frequency is zero and the coefficient
c=2cos(0)=2. Starting with both states zero, the new states are 1,4,10,20.
The final two are 20 and 10, so the raw power is 400+100−400=100, matching
(1+2+3+4)². The following records come from the executing native recurrence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/goertzel_states.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/goertzel.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const auto t=dm::algorithms::goertzel_bin_trace({1,2,3,4},0);
 std::print("| sample | input | before s1 | before s2 | evaluated s0 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 std::vector<dm::algorithms::GoertzelEvent> updates;
 for(const auto& e:t.events)if(e.kind==dm::algorithms::GoertzelEventKind::sample){
  updates.push_back(e);std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",e.index,e.input,e.before_s1,e.before_s2,e.value);
 }
 std::print("\nCoefficient {:.1f}; final states {:.1f}, {:.1f}; raw power {:.1f}; updates {}; complete records {}. Bin 1 on the same samples returns {:.12f}.\n",t.run.coefficient,t.run.s1,t.run.s2,t.run.power,t.run.sample_updates,t.events.size(),dm::algorithms::goertzel_power_bin({1,2,3,4},1));
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(62,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.4;box.height=1.6;box.font_size=22;
 for(std::size_t i=0;i<updates.size();++i){const auto& e=updates[i];const double x=4.5*double(i);
  d.node(std::format("s{}",i),{x,0},std::format("sample {}: x = {:.0f}\nnew state = {:.0f}\nolder state = {:.0f}",e.index,e.input,e.value,e.before_s1),box);
  if(i)d.edge(std::format("s{}",i-1),std::format("s{}",i),"carry");
 }
 d.text({6.75,2},"s0 = x + 2 s1 − s2; then s2 = s1 and s1 = s0",24)
  .text({6.75,-2},std::format("Terminal power = {:.0f}² + {:.0f}² − 2 × {:.0f} × {:.0f} = {:.0f}",t.run.s1,t.run.s2,t.run.s1,t.run.s2,t.run.power),22)
  .save_svg("build/goertzel_states.svg");
 if(t.run.power!=100||t.events.size()!=6)throw std::runtime_error("four-sample recurrence");

}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| sample | input | before s1 | before s2 | evaluated s0 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 4 |
| 2 | 3 | 4 | 1 | 10 |
| 3 | 4 | 10 | 4 | 20 |

Coefficient 2.0; final states 20.0, 10.0; raw power 100.0; updates 4; complete records 6. Bin 1 on the same samples returns 8.000000000000.
\image(figures/goertzel_states.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All four sample updates and both metadata records are retained. The diagram follows the actual new state and carried older state. Bin 1 has the separate hand-checkable Fourier power 8.)
\alttext(Four connected boxes show samples one through four and new states one, four, ten and twenty. The final expression uses states twenty and ten to produce raw power one hundred. A table prints every actual before-state and update.)

For a fixed angular frequency ω, the recurrence and its initial conditions are

$$
s_n=x_n+2\cos(\omega)s_{n-1}-s_{n-2},\qquad s_{-1}=s_{-2}=0.
$$
\alttext(The new state equals the current sample plus twice cosine omega times the previous state minus the older state. Both initial states are zero.)

Define q_n=s_n−exp(−iω)s_{n−1}. Substitution gives
q_n=x_n+exp(iω)q_{n−1}. Expanding this first-order relation over the finite block
shows that q differs from the Fourier sum only by a unit-magnitude phase:

$$
q_{N-1}=e^{i\omega(N-1)}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}x_j e^{-i\omega j},\qquad
|q_{N-1}|^2=s_1^2+s_2^2-cs_1s_2,\quad c=2\cos\omega.
$$
\alttext(The final complex combination is a phase factor times the finite Fourier sum. Its squared magnitude is newest state squared plus older state squared minus coefficient times their product.)

In the terminal expression, s1 and s2 name the implementation's newest and older
states, rather than sample indices. The derivation is exact arithmetic; its
floating evaluation can lose precision and even produce a finite negative value.
The output is an unnormalized squared magnitude, not mean-square signal power,
a power spectral density or calibrated watts.

The algorithm dates to [Goertzel (1958)](https://doi.org/10.2307/2310304).
[Chen’s TI SPRA066 report](https://www.ti.com/lit/an/spra066/spra066.pdf),
equations 1–4, describes the bin selection, real recurrence and magnitude-based
DTMF use. Its fixed-point DSP implementation differs from this double-valued
library. The programs here expose actual states, not a reconstructed recurrence.

> A requested frequency and a selected bin can differ

The original `goertzel_power(x,f,fs)` retains its historical rule:

$$
k=\operatorname{trunc}\!\left(0.5+\frac{Nf}{f_s}\right),\qquad
\omega=\frac{2\pi k}{N}.
$$
\alttext(The historical frequency interface truncates zero point five plus N times requested frequency divided by sampling rate, then evaluates the integer bin's angular frequency.)

For ordinary nonnegative requests this snaps to the nearest bin, with upward
half ties. Negative requests are asymmetric because truncation is towards zero;
it is not the same rule as rounding a signed number to the nearest integer.
`GoertzelFrequencyMode::unrounded` instead uses ω=2πf/fs. That avoids integer
quantization but does not make the floating arithmetic exact.

Hold a 16-sample, 16 Hz-rate signal containing a 2.4 Hz sinusoid fixed. Compare
both selectors over requested frequencies. The nearly coincident query points
immediately before the half-bin boundaries make the snapping steps visible.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/goertzel_selection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/goertzel.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const auto x=dm::algorithms::sinusoid_signal(16,16,{{2.4,1,0}});
 std::vector<double> query;
 for(int j=0;j<=25;++j)query.push_back(1+double(j)/10);
 for(double edge:{1.5,2.5,3.5})query.push_back(std::nextafter(edge,0.));
 std::sort(query.begin(),query.end());
 std::vector<double> snapped,direct;
 std::print("| requested Hz | selected bin | snapped raw power | unrounded raw power |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(double f:query){const auto a=dm::algorithms::goertzel_run(x,f,16);const auto b=dm::algorithms::goertzel_run(x,f,16,dm::algorithms::GoertzelFrequencyMode::unrounded);
  snapped.push_back(a.power);direct.push_back(b.power);std::print("| {:.17g} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",f,a.selected_bin,a.power,b.power);
 }
 const auto negative=dm::algorithms::goertzel_run({1,2,3,4},-1,4);
 std::print("\nLegacy request -1 Hz at N=4, fs=4 selects bin {}; raw power {:.1f}. Unrounded -1 Hz gives {:.12f}.\n",negative.selected_bin,negative.power,dm::algorithms::goertzel_power_at_frequency({1,2,3,4},-1,4));
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 p.line(query,snapped,"snapped",{37,99,235},2).line(query,direct,"unrounded",{5,150,105},2)
  .legend({"Legacy bin selection","Unrounded frequency"},{{37,99,235},{5,150,105}})
  .title("Fixed signal, two different frequency selectors").xlab("Requested frequency (Hz)").ylab("Raw squared-magnitude estimate")
  .x_limits(1,3.5).y_limits(0,100).save_svg("build/goertzel_selection.svg");

}
