Legacy request -1 Hz at N=4, fs=4 selects bin 0; raw power 100.0. Unrounded -1 Hz gives 8.000000000000.
\image(figures/goertzel_selection.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The blue response is constant within each selected-bin interval; the green response evaluates each supplied frequency without bin snapping. Both are responses of the same finite signal, not an estimate of an infinite sinusoid’s exact frequency.)
\alttext(A stepped blue response and a continuous-looking sampled green response differ as requests sweep from one to three point five hertz. The printed table preserves full request precision around half-bin boundaries. A separate negative-frequency example exposes offset-truncation bias.)

Finite-block response at an unrounded frequency is a finite Fourier sum, not
necessarily an integer DFT bin. For real samples, its ideal power is symmetric
in frequency. The historical signed selector can break that symmetry by choosing
a different integer; the example above makes this visible without changing the
old API's defined finite behavior.

> Reproduce the original keypad illustration

A DTMF key combines one row frequency with one column frequency. The grid is
specified by [ITU-T Q.23 (11/1988), Section 6 and Figure 1](https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-Q.23-198811-I/en).
The native generator uses that shared grid: key '5' is 770+1336 Hz, with both
amplitudes one and both phases zero. N=410 at fs=8000 gives 51.25 ms, a chosen
illustration length rather than a universal standard block.

The legacy mode requests 770 Hz but selects bin 39, about 760.98 Hz; its 1336 Hz
request selects bin 68, about 1326.83 Hz. The table prints that mapping as well as
the alternative unrounded response. The original eight legacy powers and candidate
are preserved; the two selectors need not produce equal powers.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/goertzel_dtmf.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/goertzel.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const auto x=dm::algorithms::dtmf_signal('5',410);
 const auto legacy=dm::algorithms::dtmf_peaks(x),direct=dm::algorithms::dtmf_peaks(x,8000,dm::algorithms::GoertzelFrequencyMode::unrounded);
 std::vector<double> a,b;std::vector<std::string> labels;
 std::print("| group | requested Hz | selected bin | selected Hz | legacy raw power | unrounded raw power |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(std::size_t i=0;i<8;++i){const auto& r=legacy.responses[i];a.push_back(r.power);b.push_back(direct.responses[i].power);labels.push_back(std::format("{:.0f}",legacy.target_hz[i]));
  std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",i<4?"Row":"Column",legacy.target_hz[i],r.selected_bin,double(r.selected_bin)*8000/x.size(),r.power,direct.responses[i].power);
 }
 std::print("\nLegacy peak row {:.0f} Hz, column {:.0f} Hz; candidate {}. Unrounded candidate {}. Actual updates per eight-response evaluation {}; samples {}; duration {:.2f} ms.\n",legacy.target_hz[legacy.row],legacy.target_hz[4+legacy.column],legacy.candidate,direct.candidate,legacy.sample_updates,x.size(),1000.*x.size()/8000);
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(a,labels),q=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(b,labels);
 p.title("Legacy bin selection").xlab("Requested target (Hz)").ylab("Raw power").y_limits(0,50000);
 q.title("Unrounded frequency selection").xlab("Requested target (Hz)").ylab("Raw power").y_limits(0,50000);
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(p).add(q).size(1100,510).title("Same native 770 + 1336 Hz signal, different evaluation frequencies").fit_panels().save_svg("build/goertzel_dtmf.svg");
 if(legacy.candidate!='5'||direct.candidate!='5'||legacy.sample_updates!=3280)throw std::runtime_error("original candidate");

}
