\caption(The complete four-sample history needs six records, whereas a generated four-sample signal needs four output entries. Empty metadata, disabled optional quotas, copied ownership and validation-before-quota behavior are also exercised.)
\alttext(Two bar charts compare six history records with a rejected cap of five and four generated samples with a rejected cap of three. Printed controls check empty metadata, unsafe integer conversion, nonfinite recurrence, owned copies and unchanged input.)

> What the checks establish

A four-sample calculation explains the states and terminal formula. The selected
bin table prevents requested frequencies from being mistaken for evaluated ones.
The original controlled keypad signal remains reproducible through native helpers,
while quiet and incomplete signals expose the limits of a maximum-response rule.
Known DC powers, exact direct-Fourier development fixtures and independent complex
sums check numerical behavior without using the recurrence as its own oracle.

No external dataset is needed here: controlled signals provide known frequencies,
amplitudes and deliberately incomplete cases. The shared kernel retains the old
finite results and exposes its actual work and state history. Its checked inputs
and resource bounds complement, rather than replace, the stated finite-precision
and candidate-selection limits.
